{
  "global_card_id": "london_session",
  "global_canonical": "London Session",
  "type": "merged_concept",
  "school": "ICT",
  "aliases": [
    "London",
    "London Kill Zone",
    "London Open",
    "London Killzone"
  ],
  "definition": "The European trading session kill zone (02:00–05:00 EST in ICT framing) when major institutions trade, producing high volume and volatility. In ICT methodology, London typically delivers the manipulation/sweep phase of the daily AMD cycle, raiding the prior Asia range (one side, both, or neither) and anchoring the daily wick, thereby setting up New York's distribution—whether continuation, reversal, or consolidation. In broader SMC terms, London is simply one of two high-volume windows that generate the clear market-structure swings needed for valid retracement setups.",
  "definition_per_source": {
    "Daily Profiles": "The European kill zone, active 02:00–05:00 EST. London either sweeps one side of the Asia Range and reverses at an HTF PD Array (London Reversal), sweeps both sides without sustained displacement (Seek and Destroy), or fails to reach a relevant HTF level — leaving the reversal to New York. London's behavior anchors the daily wick and determines the New York profile.",
    "Education - ICT (Updated)": "The early European kill zone (02:00–05:00 EST) that typically delivers the manipulation phase of the daily AMD cycle. London sweeps the prior Asia range (high, low, or both), establishing session highs/lows as key intraday liquidity. It sets the stage for New York distribution, either continuing or reversing direction.",
    "SMC-Supplement-PLv-X125": "The European trading session when banks, institutions, hedge funds, and traders are actively trading, producing high volume and volatility that create the market structure swings (HH/HL or LH/LL) necessary for valid Fibonacci retracement setups."
  },
  "identification_rules": [
    "Mark the London window 02:00–05:00 EST on the intraday chart; verify the chart time zone is New York/EST (Education - ICT notes optionally extending to 08:30 to avoid blank space before the AM session). SMC-Supplement instead defines the session loosely by European market hours and recommends trading London open through New York close continuously.",
    "Identify the prior Asia session range high and low (Education - ICT specifies 20:00–00:00 EST) and observe whether London sweeps one side, both sides, or stays inside.",
    "Confirm a London manipulation/reversal when price sweeps an extreme and then closes through the opposing candle series (Change in the State of Delivery), often with aggressive displacement back into the Asia range or SMT divergence against a correlated asset.",
    "Classify a London Reversal only when London sweeps one side, engages a relevant HTF PD Array (order block, FVG, or liquidity level), and reverses without sweeping the opposite side.",
    "If London sweeps both Asia sides without sustained displacement, classify the day as Seek and Destroy (consolidation expected for New York).",
    "If London creates most of the daily range via large one-directional expansion, treat it as a second Seek and Destroy / consolidation condition, since limited range remains for New York.",
    "If London moves directionally or consolidates but does not reach a relevant HTF PD Array/key level, classify it as a London failure (Daily Profiles) or accumulation (Education - ICT) — flag a potential New York Reversal/Manipulation where NY later sweeps London's range.",
    "Mark London session high, low, and optionally equilibrium (0.5) as key intraday liquidity reference levels for New York.",
    "Mark the London manipulation leg (from the swept extreme to the opposing extreme that created it) for subsequent standard deviation projections (Education - ICT).",
    "Note that an up-close or down-close candle at the swept London extreme can form an order block serving as a New York point of interest (Education - ICT).",
    "Confirm the session is genuinely active by observing clear market structure swings (defined HH/HL or LH/LL) and impulse moves strong enough to break prior structure, not sluggish sideways action (SMC-Supplement, for Fibonacci validity)."
  ],
  "common_mistakes": [
    "Trading directional breakouts out of the London range on a Seek and Destroy day (both sides swept) instead of fading moves back into the range.",
    "Expecting New York to produce a large directional move when London has already created most of the daily range, leading to low-probability entries.",
    "Entering on the initial London sweep without waiting for a confirmed Change in the State of Delivery (closure through opposing candles) and aggressive displacement back into the range.",
    "Misclassifying a London Reversal when London forms a swing high/low but never engaged a relevant HTF PD Array — this is liquidity to be swept by New York, not a true reversal point.",
    "Acting on a Change in the State of Delivery during London without confirming an HTF level was reached; such signals can be false, letting price run highs/lows again into New York.",
    "Ignoring SMT divergence against a correlated asset when assessing whether a London high/low will hold (Education - ICT).",
    "Treating the London session as the distribution move rather than the manipulation phase before New York delivers the real move (Education - ICT).",
    "Misaligning the chart time zone (not using New York/EST), causing incorrect marking of the 02:00–05:00 window.",
    "Ignoring high-impact news context (CPI, FOMC, NFP) from the prior day, which often produces Seek and Destroy London behavior (Education - ICT).",
    "Trading New York when the drawn liquidity target (e.g., previous day high) was already taken during Asia or London (Education - ICT).",
    "Relying solely on the London level as a target when it does not provide minimum 2R, instead of extending with standard deviation projections; or failing to project the manipulation leg from the correct swing extreme (Education - ICT).",
    "Expecting a London reversal when daily structure shows expansion-met-with-expansion (a true reversal candle) rather than a manipulation wick (Education - ICT).",
    "Trading Fibonacci setups outside the London or New York high-volume windows, where low volume causes sluggish price action and frequent fakeouts (SMC-Supplement).",
    "Failing to distinguish genuine session-driven momentum swings from choppy, range-bound action that can occur even during session hours due to news (SMC-Supplement)."
  ],
  "trading_implication": "ICT sources (Daily Profiles, Education - ICT) treat London as the manipulation leg of the daily AMD cycle: anticipate a sweep of the Asia range during 02:00–05:00 EST and confirm a reversal via Change in the State of Delivery and SMT divergence. A directional sweep engaging an HTF level signals a London Reversal with New York continuing in that direction; a failure to reach an HTF level signals a New York Reversal/Manipulation. Education - ICT recommends entering on a lower time frame after confirmation, targeting previous day high/low or -2 to -2.5 standard deviation projections of the London manipulation leg, and standing aside if drawn liquidity is already taken. On Seek and Destroy days (both sides swept, or London exhausts the daily range), fade moves back into the range toward equilibrium (0.5) and the opposite extreme. SMC-Supplement instead uses London purely as one of two high-volume windows for executing Fibonacci retracements, drawing valid structure to target the 0.5–0.618 gold zone, and recommends trading only from London open through New York close.",
  "merge_notes": "Two ICT-school sources (Daily Profiles, Education - ICT) share the 02:00–05:00 EST kill-zone framing and AMD/manipulation model with detailed sweep/reversal classifications. SMC-Supplement-PLv-X125 is General school (single_source) and conceives London much more loosely — defined by European market hours rather than a fixed window — used only to justify a high-volume environment for Fibonacci retracement (0.5–0.618 gold zone) trades, with no Asia-range sweep, AMD, or HTF PD Array logic. The continuous London-open-through-NY-close trading window is unique to SMC-Supplement and conflicts with the ICT sources' tightly bounded session and stand-aside discipline.",
